`;

const body = `
<h2 id="gas-blower">The gas blower was never mine</h2>
<p>My husband Walt ran the yard for 29 years. When he passed, he left me a garage full of tools I couldn't use.</p>
<p>The worst was the gas blower. A big orange thing. I could barely lift it off the shelf. One Saturday I pulled that cord eleven times. It coughed once and quit. My shoulder ached for a week.</p>
<p>So the blower went back on the shelf. And the broom came out.</p>
<p>At first it was fine. A sweep before coffee. Another after lunch. I told myself it was good exercise.</p>
<p>But the porch doesn't care how you feel. It fills back up every single day. Needles. Seeds. Leaves. Grit. Some mornings my back said no, and I skipped a day. Then two. By Friday the needles were in my front hall, carried in on my own shoes.</p>
${fig("/images/wr/gas-blower-garage.webp", "A dusty orange gas leaf blower on a garage floor among fallen leaves", 900, 505, "Walt's blower. Ten pounds and a pull cord I couldn't win against.")}

<h2 id="tried">Everything I tried</h2>
<p><strong>A better broom.</strong> I bought a stiff push broom at the hardware store. It moved more leaves. It also pushed more dust right back into the cracks.</p>
<p><strong>My son.</strong> Mike lives 40 minutes away. He'd come on Saturdays with Walt's blower and do the porch in ten minutes flat. I hated asking. And leaves don't fall on Saturdays. They fall every day.</p>
<p><strong>A yard service.</strong> The young men with the big backpack blowers. The company that makes the blower I use now says a service like that can cost $50 to $100 a visit. Every visit. On their schedule, not mine.</p>
<p>Nothing fit. The broom was too slow. The gas blower was too big. And help was never there on the day the leaves came down.</p>

<h2 id="saturday">The Saturday it got to me</h2>
<p>One Saturday in late September, Mike finished the porch, put Walt's blower away, and sat down next to me on the step.</p>
<p>“Mom,” he said. “Maybe it's time to think about a smaller place.”</p>
<p>He meant well. He always does.</p>
<p>But I raised that boy on this porch. And I was not going to give it up over pine needles.</p>
<p>That night I couldn't sleep. So I did what my granddaughter taught me. I looked it up.</p>

<h2 id="strangers">What strangers told me</h2>
<p>I didn't search for a gadget. I searched for people like me. People who still do their own porch and had stopped sweeping.</p>
<p>I found them in the reviews of one small blower. Some of them could have written my life.</p>
