<h2 id="broom-trap">The Broom Trap</h2>
<p>Here is what nobody ever told me.</p>
<p><strong>The porch didn't get harder. I had the wrong tools.</strong></p>
<p>A broom only pushes. It drags the mess along the boards. The big stuff moves. The fine stuff, the grit and needle bits and pollen, drops into the cracks and the grain of the wood. So you sweep, and it looks clean, and by noon it's back. You're not cleaning the porch. You're moving the mess around on it.</p>
<p>Try it tomorrow. Sweep one step, then run a finger along the crack between two boards. That gray line on your fingertip is what the broom left behind.</p>
<p>One buyer said it perfectly:</p>
${q("I was sweeping out my garage and leaving dust streaks everywhere. I pulled out my little blower and the whole garage was dust free in less than two minutes!", TP(5, "28 Sep 2026"))}
<p>Dust streaks everywhere. That was my porch, every day.</p>
<p>And the big blower is the opposite mistake. It's built to push a whole yard of leaves, so it's heavy. The brand behind the one I use now puts full-size blowers at 10 pounds and up. The gas ones need a pull cord. Getting one out, started and put away takes longer than the porch.</p>
<p>That's the Broom Trap. A five-minute job, stuck between a tool that's too weak and a tool that's too big.</p>
${fig("/images/wr/broom-vs-gas-split.webp", "Left: a straw broom on porch steps covered in leaves. Right: an orange gas leaf blower on a garage floor", 900, 505)}
